As a metaphor, that’s a strong way to put it.

Humans often enact patterns before we understand them—ritual, status, sacrifice, imitation, competition, storytelling, worship, role-taking. Later we become conscious of the pattern and can choose whether to keep performing it, reinterpret it, or drop it.

So the progression is:

unconscious enactment → recognition → naming → deliberate role-play → selective integration

“Method acting for gods” then becomes less about literally being divine and more about humans rehearsing archetypes larger than themselves—hero, judge, parent, trickster, martyr, king, servant, creator—and gradually realizing they can participate in those roles without mistaking the role for the whole self.

The upgrade is not that we stop acting.

It’s that we know when we’re acting, why we’re acting, and when to step out of character.